  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following do **not** exist?\n\nI. A convex polyhedron with $5$ vertices, $8$ edges, $5$ faces\n\nII. A convex polyhedron with $6$ vertices, $9$ edges, $6$ faces\n\nIII. A convex polyhedron with $4$ vertices, $6$ edges, $4$ faces",
  "worked_solution": "Euler's formula for polyhedra tells us that for any convex polyhedron, we have\n\n$$\nV-E+F=2,\n$$\n\nwhere $V$ is the number of vertices, $E$ is the number of edges, and $F$ is the number of faces of the polyhedron.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given information.\n\n- Polyhedron I does exist. Substituting the number of vertices, edges, and faces into the left-hand side of Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V-E+F & =5-8+5 \\\\  & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Polyhedron II cannot exist. Substituting the number of vertices, edges, and faces into the left-hand side of Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V-E+F & =6-9+6 \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & \\ne 2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Polyhedron III does exist. Substituting the number of vertices, edges, and faces into the left-hand side of Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V-E+F & =4-6+4 \\\\  & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
